# Hotel Booking API

![Version](https://img.shields.io/badge/version-1.0.0-blue)
![Laravel](https://img.shields.io/badge/Laravel-13-red)
![PHP](https://img.shields.io/badge/PHP-8.3-777bb4)

A backend API for a hotel booking platform, built with Laravel 13. This project was built as a hands-on exercise to relearn backend fundamentals after a year away from active development — focused on getting the core architecture, security, and business logic right rather than shipping every possible feature at once.

## Tech Stack

- **Framework:** Laravel 13
- **Primary database:** PostgreSQL
- **Logging database:** MongoDB (system errors + business activity audit trail)
- **Queue / Cache:** Redis
- **Authentication:** JWT (`tymon/jwt-auth`)
- **Payment Gateway:** Midtrans (Snap)
- **Password hashing:** Argon2id

## Architecture

This project follows a layered architecture rather than putting logic directly in controllers:

```
Route → Middleware → Controller → Request (validation) → Service (business logic)
→ Repository (database queries) → Model → back up through Resource (response shaping)
```

- **Repository + Interface** — isolates database queries behind an interface, so services depend on an abstraction rather than a concrete Eloquent implementation.
- **Service** — owns business logic and orchestrates one or more repositories/other services. Controllers stay thin.
- **Custom Exceptions** — business-rule violations (e.g. a room being unavailable, a booking that can't be cancelled) are thrown as specific exception classes, each with its own HTTP status code via a `render()` method.

## Key Features

- JWT authentication with refresh tokens, email verification, and password reset (Mailtrap-tested)
- Role-based access control (`admin` / `client`) via a custom Role system (not a third-party package)
- Race-condition-safe booking: room availability is enforced with database transactions and row-level locking, not just application-level checks
- Auto room assignment — clients book a room _type_, the system assigns the first available physical room (like a hotel receptionist would)
- Booking reschedule with automatic payment re-issuance when the price changes
- Midtrans Snap integration with webhook signature verification and idempotent notification handling
- Scheduled auto-expiry for unpaid pending bookings
- Review system with ownership checks and a profanity filter
- Structured error logging to MongoDB, with a generic error message returned to clients (no internal details ever leak in API responses)
- Argon2id password hashing with automatic rehash-on-login for any legacy hashes

## Prerequisites

- PHP 8.3+
- Composer
- PostgreSQL
- MongoDB
- Redis
- WSL (if developing on Windows)

## Installation

```bash
git clone <this-repo-url>
cd booking-hotel
make setup-packages
make install
```

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` (done automatically by `make install`) and fill in:

```env
DB_DATABASE=hotel_booking
DB_USERNAME=your_pg_user
DB_PASSWORD=your_pg_password

MONGODB_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017
MONGODB_DATABASE=hotel_booking_logs

MIDTRANS_SERVER_KEY=SB-Mid-server-xxxxxxxxxxxxx
MIDTRANS_CLIENT_KEY=SB-Mid-client-xxxxxxxxxxxxx

MAIL_MAILER=smtp
MAIL_HOST=sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io
MAIL_USERNAME=your_mailtrap_username
MAIL_PASSWORD=your_mailtrap_password
```

Then run migrations and seed the database:

```bash
make fresh-m options=--seed
```

This creates:

- 1 default admin account (see below)
- 4 sample hotels with room types and rooms
- 3 sample reviews with varied ratings (for testing `/hotels/recommended`)

### Default admin account (local development only)

```
Email:    admin@hotelbooking.test
Password: password
```

This account is created by `AdminSeeder` and only exists in whatever database you seed locally — it's safe to keep in version control for that reason. **This seeder should never be run against a production database.** For creating an admin on a real server, use `php artisan admin:create` instead, which prompts for credentials interactively and never stores them anywhere.

## Running the app

You need **three** processes running simultaneously during development:

```bash
make run-s   # HTTP server
make run-q   # Queue worker (required for emails and webhook side-effects)
```

The scheduler (`bookings:expire`, runs hourly) needs a cron entry to actually fire on a schedule:

```bash
* * * * * cd /path/to/project && php artisan schedule:run >> /dev/null 2>&1
```

Without it, you can still run `php artisan bookings:expire` manually at any time.

## API Documentation

See the Postman collection: https://documenter.getpostman.com/view/33217412/2sBYB1PUTJ

## Testing

Automated tests are not yet part of this project (see Roadmap). All testing so far has been done manually via Postman.

## Roadmap

Things intentionally deferred past this initial version, in no particular priority order:

- [ ] Automated tests (Pest) + CI pipeline
- [ ] Dockerize the full stack (app, Postgres, MongoDB, Redis)
- [ ] Deploy to a real environment
- [ ] Redis-backed response caching (currently Redis is only used for the queue)
- [ ] OpenAPI/Swagger spec generation
- [ ] Email confirmation field (re-type email on register) + switch email verification from link-based to OTP-based
- [ ] Transaction-level OTP (SMS/email) as a separate concept from account/email verification
- [ ] QRIS as an additional payment method alongside Midtrans Snap
- [ ] `is_active` flag for hotels/room types/rooms (soft-deactivation instead of hard delete, for contract-ended scenarios)
- [ ] A "force delete" flow for hotels/room types that cascades through empty child records while still blocking on active bookings
- [ ] Additional roles (e.g. hotel owner/receptionist) with hotel-scoped authorization
- [ ] `rooms_count` on the Room Type resource
- [ ] Guest capacity validation on booking (currently `room_types.capacity` is stored but not enforced)

## Version History

- **1.0.0** — Initial release: full CRUD for hotels/room types/rooms, JWT auth with email verification and password reset, booking with race-condition-safe auto room assignment, reschedule, cancellation, Midtrans payment integration, review system, admin user management, and MongoDB-based error/activity logging.
